# =============================================================================
#  .gitignore - the PowerShell station's transport repo
# =============================================================================
#  NOTE: ops-logs/*.txt is deliberately NOT ignored. The captured logs are the
#  whole point of this repo - publishing them is how a run escapes a machine
#  nobody can reach. Don't "tidy" them into here.
#
#  This ships as `gitignore`, without the dot, so that it governs the TRANSPORT
#  repo rather than the repo carrying it. The bootstrap restores the dot on the
#  way out. Do not "fix" the name.
# =============================================================================

# =============================================================================
#  BEGIN SHARED BLOCK - identical in station/bash/gitignore and
#  station/powershell/gitignore, and a test in tests/test-doc-coherence.sh
#  refuses a change to one that is not made to the other.
#
#  Both payloads can be planted into the SAME transport repo, and bootstrap
#  never overwrites: whichever flavour is planted first supplies the .gitignore
#  that then governs the other. A rule that existed in only one of them would
#  therefore be present or absent depending on the order somebody typed two
#  commands in - and the rules below are the ones that keep a token out of a
#  repository that gets cloned onto a locked-down control node.
# =============================================================================
# --- secrets: never, under any circumstances ---------------------------------
.git-token
.heliograph-passphrase
*passphrase*
pat.txt
*.pat
.env
.env.*
*.pem
*.key
*.pfx
*.p12
*.jks
id_rsa
id_rsa.*
id_ed25519
id_ed25519.*
*credential*
*secret*.json
*secrets*.yml
*secrets*.yaml
kubeconfig
.kube/

# --- terraform ---------------------------------------------------------------
# State can hold secrets in plaintext and is never this repo's to own.
.terraform/
.terraform.lock.hcl
*.tfstate
*.tfstate.*
*.tfvars
*.tfvars.json
crash.log
crash.*.log
tfplan
*.tfplan

# --- ansible -----------------------------------------------------------------
*.retry
.vault_pass
vault_pass.txt
fact_cache/

# --- python ------------------------------------------------------------------
__pycache__/
*.py[cod]
.venv/
venv/
.pytest_cache/

# --- scratch -----------------------------------------------------------------
scratch/
tmp/
*.tmp
*.bak
*.orig
*.swp
*~

# --- editors / OS ------------------------------------------------------------
.vscode/
.idea/
.DS_Store
Thumbs.db

# --- agent state (local to each control node, never shared) ------------------
.station.lock
.station-state
# The step hashes `./station.sh --pin` approved. LOCAL on purpose: an approval
# recorded in this repo could be edited from the far side, which is the only
# side a pin exists to distrust.
.station-approved
# The same, for station.ps1. A SEPARATE FILE because each --pin truncates
# before writing and the two payloads approve different files, so sharing one
# would mean each implementation silently unapproved the other's.
.station-approved-ps
# How the runner tells the loop whether the finished log actually got out, so
# the loop can publish `idle` or `undelivered`. Local: it describes this
# machine's last delivery attempt and means nothing to anybody else. Both
# run.sh and run.ps1 write it, under the same name.
.station-delivery
# The transport's variables for a detached service - see service.sh. Mode 600,
# AND IT HOLDS A TOKEN, which is why it is in the shared block rather than
# beside the bash-only state below: only service.sh writes one today, but a
# rule that keeps a credential out of a repository must not depend on which
# payload somebody planted first.
.station-env
# The same, for the PowerShell payload's service.ps1. A SEPARATE FILE because
# the bash one is a bash script that gets sourced and this one is KEY=value
# that gets read - one name for two formats would mean whichever station read
# it last was wrong. It holds a token too.
.station-env-ps
# =============================================================================
#  END SHARED BLOCK
# =============================================================================

# --- station.ps1's own state -------------------------------------------------
# PowerShell writes a transcript beside a scheduled task when one is configured.
# It can carry whatever a step printed before redaction reached it.
*.transcript
